#
# prima-materia — Kabbalah
#
# Opened from the ordered harvest of prima-materia#924: registered at
# pm:corpusNamed in traditions/registry-west-asia.ttl with no node. Four works
# of Ashlag are opened, each read complete in the Hebrew: the Petiḥa
# le-Ḥokhmat ha-Qabbala, harvested in traditions/kabbalah-petiha.ttl, the
# Haqdama le-Sefer ha-Zohar, harvested in traditions/kabbalah-haqdama-zohar.ttl,
# the Haqdama le-Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot with Histaklut Penimit to Part 1,
# harvested in traditions/kabbalah-haqdama-tes.ttl, and the essays Matan Tora,
# Ha-Arvut and Ha-Shalom of Kuntres Matan Torah, harvested in
# traditions/kabbalah-matan-tora.ttl.
# The works the registration named, the further works of Ashlag listed in
# #924 and the other pieces of Kuntres Matan Torah stay unopened and are
# declared below.
#
# Order of the file: the works read; the unopened works; the tradition with
# the three decisions of SPEC §14.
#

#
# The works read
#

pmw:PetihaLeHokhmatHaQabbala a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Yehuda Leib Ashlag, Petiḥa le-Ḥokhmat ha-Qabbala, an opening to Kabbalistic wisdom"@en, "Jehuda Leib Aschlag, Petiḥa le-Ḥokhmat ha-Qabbala, Einleitung in die Weisheit der Kabbala"@de ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-11-05 in the Hebrew, sections 1 to 188, in the text of the Sefaria library, version Zohar with Sulam commentary, Jerusalem 1945, whose version source Sefaria gives as the National Library of Israel record NNL_ALEPH001329911. The text layer has no title page; the edition is therefore taken from Sefaria's version record and not from a title page, and every node says so. Section 99 is absent from the numbering itself: the Hebrew passes from 98 to 100, and so does the Bnei Baruch English. The layer keeps the author's headings and his references to his diagrams (the Tree, figures 3 to 12) but not the diagrams. Cross-checked against the Hebrew of the Bnei Baruch edition, which keeps the abbreviations of the print."@en ;
    skos:note "Two translations by two translators. Avi Steinhart, The Sefaria Sulam (2023, CC-BY), covers sections 1 to 45 only. The English of Bnei Baruch, translator unnamed, copyrighted, read for understanding only, covers the whole work; it was taken from the Bnei Baruch media archive, content unit kB3eD83I. A German version of Bnei Baruch exists and was not used for the comparison. For sections 46 to 188 only one translator was reachable, and the nodes rest on the Hebrew; definitions are the harvest's own paraphrase, checked against both English texts for any shared run of six words."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvested under prima-materia#924 with two independent passes over the Hebrew and a reconciliation. The first pass wrote 265 records with full fields, the second 313 short ones. Ten records of the second pass had no counterpart in the first by offset within 1500 characters and a word overlap of at least a fifth; read at their places, one was owed and added (the summary of what is new in the world of Nekudim, §112), six were covered by records of the first pass at the same or the neighbouring section, and three were citations (of the Tikkunei Zohar, the Zohar Hadash and Scripture) that the author interprets and that are not owed. The second pass counted eleven receivings in all; every one is a citation of Talmud, Midrash, Zohar, Scripture or Sefer Yetzira read by the author, and none claims that the wording of the work comes from another speaker, so no pm:Receiving is written."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvest counts: 283 nodes, 208 concepts (among them 7 capacities, 17 conditions of failure, 1 sign of success, 1 dispute entered as a concept, and 18 nodes written by the integrator, 17 members of series and one added from the reconciliation), 3 practices, 6 yieldings, 9 prerequisites (7 at stated necessity, 2 at prescribed order), 7 cautions, 27 namings, 10 series and 13 orderings. One sign of success against seventeen conditions of failure. The one sign, the revelation of the King Messiah when the end of Atzilut comes to equal the end of Adam Kadmon, is announced for the future and is not a sign a practitioner could observe; most of the conditions of failure are cosmological — vessels that break, lights that depart — and six bear on human conduct: the husks and the wicked called dead for receiving in phase four itself (§16), a life wholly for oneself (§57), deeds of the lower ones that can blemish the back vessels of Zeʿir Anpin and Nukva (§126), Adam ha-Rishon's transgression (§151), the spoiling of deeds that makes the worlds descend again (§162), and the dependence of every light above the six extremities on deeds (§176). The work prescribes few practices: Torah and commandments done in order to give contentment to the Maker, the rehearsing of its three foundations, and good deeds that raise MaN."@en .

pmw:HaqdamaLeSeferHaZohar a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ashlag, Haqdama le-Sefer ha-Zohar, Introduction to the Book of the Zohar"@en, "Aschlag, Haqdama le-Sefer ha-Zohar, Einleitung in das Buch Sohar"@de ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-11-06 in the Hebrew, sections 1 to 71 (alef to ayin-alef), in the text of the Sefaria library, version Baal HaSulam's Introduction to Zohar, ed. Zohar HaSulam, 2019, 169 paragraphs. The text layer has no title page; the edition is taken from Sefaria's version record and not from a title page, and every node says so. The layer adds what the print does not have: section headings run into the paragraphs, abbreviations spelled out in parentheses, Hebrew renderings after the Aramaic of the Zohar and the Tikkunim, partial vocalisation, and at §64 an editor's Hebrew paraphrase of Berakhot 20a merged into the text; that paraphrase is not the author's and no node rests on it."@en ;
    skos:note "Cross-read against a second Hebrew witness, the Bnei Baruch text (media archive content unit ALlyoveA, file AmFo68Hl.docx), which keeps the abbreviations and more of the author's cross-references and lacks the editorial layer. A word comparison with vocalisation, punctuation and parentheses removed agrees at a ratio of 0.913; the differences are spelling, abbreviation against full form, headings and cross-references, and none changes a claim entered. Minor variants: §18 le-nitzḥiyyut against la-netzaḥ, §51 she-ibbed against meʾabbed, §65 ʿerekh hafkhi against ʿerekh hafukh, §71 the Zohar Naso cited by paragraph in the one and by folio in the other, and §70 the Aramaic of the Tikkunim kept where the Sefaria text has the verse."@en ;
    skos:note "Two translations by two translators, both complete and both from the Sefaria library, read for understanding only: Yoel Finkelman, The True Wisdom (2024, CC-BY-NC), and Baruch Cohen (2019, CC0). The translators are taken from Sefaria's version records, not from a title page. Definitions are the harvest's own paraphrase, checked against both English texts for any shared run of six words."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvested under prima-materia#924 with two independent passes over the Hebrew and a reconciliation. The first pass wrote 200 records with full fields, the second 273 short ones. Sixteen records of the second pass had no counterpart in the first by offset within 1500 characters and a word overlap of at least a fifth; read at their places, none was owed and missing: thirteen were covered by records of the first pass at the same or a later section, where the author answers what he asks at §§1-3 or restates at §§24-25 and 71 what he said before, and three were not owed (the lists of five questions and six inquiries, which frame the work and are answered where entered, and the author's remark at §57 that grief keeps him from saying more). Reading the whole text, the integrator added eight nodes from claims the second pass held and the first held only inside other records: two prerequisites with stated consequence (§§15, 34), two capacities (§§30, 33), three yieldings (§§58, 67, 71) and one condition of failure (§58). Neither pass counted a receiving: the work quotes Talmud, Midrash, Zohar, Tikkunim, Maimonides and Scripture and interprets them, and claims no other speaker for its own words, so no pm:Receiving is written."@en ;
    skos:note "Compared with the harvest of the Petiḥa by the same author before writing: 23 records of the first pass carry claims a Petiḥa node already carries (the purpose of creation, pleasure measured by the will, the will as the one thing created from nothing, separation by disparity of form, the husks called dead, the practice of Torah and commandments for the Maker's sake and its yielding of the turned will and of adhesion, the five worlds named one by one, the inverse order of lights and vessels) and are not written again; links point at the Petiḥa nodes. Eleven nodes state a claim of the Petiḥa in a different form and carry a note beginning Differs from."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvest counts: 178 nodes, 99 concepts (among them 33 capacities, 13 conditions of failure, 4 signs of success and 4 disputes entered as concepts), 15 practices, 20 yieldings, 13 prerequisites (10 at stated necessity, 3 at prescribed order), 9 cautions, 11 namings, 7 series and 4 orderings. Four signs of success against thirteen conditions of failure. Of the four signs, two are inward and announced in advance (the longing that does not let one sleep, §31; the guest's word that all was made for him, §40), one is a light announced to shine in the body (§44), and one is reported from the Talmud of a past case (the rain that came when Rav Yehuda took off a shoe, §64). Unlike the Petiḥa, most conditions of failure bear on human conduct: correction only in its time through suffering (§16), the will widening until death (§29), the snare of the impure maidservant (§31), impermanent purification not accepted and lights lost when the will wakes again (§51), the generation's darkness from neglect of the secrets of Torah (§57), resting on the rungs of the commentary (§58), the exterior raised over the interior (§§67, 69, 71) and commandments done without knowledge and for oneself (§70). The work prescribes many practices, a graded ladder of them: commandments even without intention, the 613 in deed, commandments with the desired intention, the Torah's secrets and the commandments' reasons, each commandment by its true intention, with the study of the Zohar through the author's commentary and the raising of the inner Torah as the practices of its last chapters."@en .

pmw:HaqdamaLeTalmudEserHaSefirot a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ashlag, Haqdama le-Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot, with Histaklut Penimit"@en, "Aschlag, Haqdama le-Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot, mit Histaklut Penimit"@de ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-11-07 in the Hebrew, in two units: the Introduction, sections 1 to 156 (alef to qof-nun-vav), 443 segments in the text layer, and Histaklut Penimit to Part 1, chapters 1 to 10, 36 paragraphs of the Sefaria segmentation; in the text of the Sefaria library, version Jerusalem, 1956-1966, whose version source Sefaria gives as the National Library of Israel record NNL_ALEPH990005320620205171 (Public Domain). The text layer has no title page; the edition is taken from Sefaria's version record and not from a title page, and every node says so. Section 71 (ayin-alef) is absent from the author's numbering: the text passes from 70 to 72, and so does the Bnei Baruch Hebrew. It is a gap in the numbering of the print and not a loss of the text layer: §70 closes the dependent love with its second measure and §72 opens the independent love, as the author's order of the four measures requires. The Introduction's quotations of the Tikkunim carry bracketed Hebrew renderings of the Aramaic, which are the edition's and not the author's; Histaklut Penimit runs its paragraph headings into the body without a break. The Petiḥa refers to the work at sections 1, 20 and 111, the Haqdama le-Sefer ha-Zohar at §§13 and 34."@en ;
    skos:note "Cross-read against a second Hebrew witness, the Bnei Baruch text (kabbalahmedia.info). For the Introduction a word comparison with punctuation, parentheses and brackets removed agrees at a ratio of 0.9999 (19,791 against 19,795 words); the one difference is the title line, and none touches a claim entered. For Histaklut Penimit the Bnei Baruch Hebrew is a PDF whose text layer extracts only as font codes, so that unit rests on the Sefaria witness alone."@en ;
    skos:note "Translations, read for understanding only and counted by translator. For the Introduction two: The Sefaria Sulam (2023), translators Avi Steinhart and Yaacov David Shulman, from Sefaria's version record, and the English and German of Bnei Baruch, translator unnamed, copyrighted. For Histaklut Penimit one: the Bnei Baruch English and German. Definitions are the harvest's own paraphrase, checked against all of these for any shared run of six words, and the Hebrew terms against both Hebrew witnesses for any run of six words."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvested under prima-materia#924 with two independent passes over the Hebrew and a reconciliation. The first pass wrote 268 records for the Introduction and 107 for Histaklut Penimit with full fields, the second 310 and 110 short ones. Records of the second pass with no counterpart in the first by offset within 1500 characters and a word overlap of at least a fifth: 23 and 3. Read at their places, in the Introduction one was owed and added (the measure of the wicked in a person, the uncorrected years of concealment, which have no share after death, §127), 21 were covered by records of the first pass at the same or a neighbouring section, and one was a proof text and not owed (no blade of grass below without its mazal above, §144); in Histaklut Penimit two were covered and one was a proof verse. Reading the whole text, the integrator added twelve nodes more: a condition of failure at §3 (life without Torah harder than death), the four measures of love, the three members sod, remez and peshat, three steps of the order of study, and the practice of studying without materialising at §156, so that the series and the prerequisite there have their members; he dropped five yieldings of the first pass that ran from capacity to capacity without a practice and four series that repeated another series or ordering or had fewer than three members. Neither pass found a receiving the work owes: the first proposed the Ari's words at the head of Histaklut Penimit, which are a quotation from Ez Ḥayyim and a compilation, not a claim that the work's own wording comes from another speaker, so no pm:Receiving is written."@en ;
    skos:note "Compared with the harvests of the Petiḥa and of the Haqdama le-Sefer ha-Zohar by the same author before writing: 35 records of the first pass, 9 of the Introduction and 26 of Histaklut Penimit, carry claims that 27 nodes of those files already carry (among them the purpose of creation, the will to receive as the one thing created from nothing and as the whole matter of the creature, separation by disparity of form, the soul as a portion of God, the four phases of direct light and the letters of the Name, the husks and the wicked called dead, Torah and commandments for the Maker's sake with its yielding of the turned will and adhesion, lishma and the indwelling of the Shekhina, the dry Torah of the Tikkunim, and the Ari); they are not written again, and links point at those nodes. Thirty-nine nodes state a claim of those files in a different form and carry a note beginning Differs from."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvest counts: 319 nodes, 160 concepts (among them 33 capacities, 27 conditions of failure, 17 signs of success and 10 disputes entered as concepts), 33 practices, 33 yieldings, 30 prerequisites (25 at stated necessity, 5 at prescribed order), 33 cautions, 15 namings, 10 series and 5 orderings; 254 nodes rest on the Introduction and 65 on Histaklut Penimit. Seventeen signs of success against twenty-seven conditions of failure. The signs are announced in advance and are mostly inward: delight in each commandment and bitterness in each sin (§55), the Knower of secrets testifying by the disclosure of the Face (§56), the secrets of Torah opening like a spring (§25), rejoicing over all past suffering (§107), the answers of the tables lying ready as in a box (§156). The conditions of failure bear almost all on conduct, and the work sets a deadline that can be counted: whoever has seen no good sign in his study within five years, or three after R. Yose, will not see one (§§23, 25); the light fails for want of faith (§§12-13), for neglect of the quantity or the quality of study (§18), and in revealed Torah whose garments are too material (§22); one sin loses all attainment (§117); one who does not grieve with the community dies an intermediate (§128); the author's own first commentary is said to have been nearly in vain (§156). The work prescribes many practices, an order of them from the Mishna's way of sorrow and study with faith not yet for its own sake, through toil in Torah to find the Face and grief with the community, to the order of study of Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot; Histaklut Penimit is an account of the will to receive, the restriction and the four phases, and of its 65 nodes 28 are concepts that are neither capacities nor conditions nor signs."@en .

pmw:MatanToraHaArvutHaShalom a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ashlag, the essays Matan Tora, Ha-Arvut and Ha-Shalom"@en, "Aschlag, die Aufsätze Matan Tora, Ha-Arvut und Ha-Schalom"@de ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-11-08 in the Hebrew: three essays of Kuntres Matan Torah, Matan Tora, sections 1 to 16 (alef to tet-zayin), 38 segments in the text layer; Ha-Arvut, sections 17 to 29 (yod-zayin to kaf-tet), 42 segments; and Ha-Shalom, sections 1 to 20 (alef to kaf), 80 segments, subtitled a scientific study on an experiential basis concerning the obligation of the service of God. The text is that of the Sefaria library, version Sefer Matan Torah, ed. Zohar HaSulam, 2019, whose version source Sefaria gives only as sefaria.org, with the licence unknown; Sefaria's index files the collection under Kabbalah, Baal HaSulam, and dates the composition 1931-1935, and there the essays are the First, Second and Fifth Discourse. The text layer has no title page, so the edition is taken from Sefaria's version record and not from a title page, and every node says so. Ha-Arvut carries on the numbering of Matan Tora: an editorial footnote of the edition, removed from the text before reading, says that Ha-Arvut was printed in the second Kuntres as the continuation of Matan Tora with continuous numbering. Ha-Shalom numbers its sections afresh from alef. The layer runs the section headings into the paragraphs and vocalises the quotations. It has small slips of spacing and spelling, for example the reference at Ha-Arvut §25 to Matan Tora §16, and none of them touches a claim entered."@en ;
    skos:note "Only one Hebrew witness was used, the Sefaria text. No second Hebrew witness was compared in this run, so this harvest does not know how far other printings differ."@en ;
    skos:note "Translations, read for understanding only and counted by translator. The first is Vital Transformation and Michal Ricardo (2023, CC-BY), complete for all three essays. The second is the Sefaria Community Translation (CC0), which is partial: 31 of the 38 segments of Matan Tora, 37 of the 42 of Ha-Arvut, and none of Ha-Shalom. The translators are taken from Sefaria's version records, not from a title page. For Ha-Shalom only one translator was reachable here; its nodes rest on the Hebrew, read with that one English text. Definitions are the harvest's own paraphrase, checked against both English texts for any shared run of six words."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvested under prima-materia#924 with two independent passes over the Hebrew and a reconciliation. The first pass wrote 169 records with full fields, the second 146 short ones. Two records of the second pass had no counterpart in the first by offset within 1500 characters and a word overlap of at least a fifth. Read at their places, neither was owed and missing. Love of the neighbour as the sum of the Torah (Matan Tora §1) is the question the author answers at §15, where the first pass held it. Rabbi Elazar's agreement with the sages (Ha-Arvut §21) restates the reconciliation the first pass held at §§17-19. Reading the whole text, the integrator made several corrections to the first pass. Sixteen records that it marked as mediated by the Talmud, the Midrash, Maimonides, the Tosafot, the Zohar or the kabbalists are the author's readings of works he quotes; where entered, they are textual attestation of this work, since nothing was read through another work. Eight disputes whose other side the author only reports are entered as concepts: R. Elazar and the sages, Ibn Ezra after R. Marinus, formal philosophy, the four systems of providence and the communal society of Russia. Of the three orderings it marked as series, two became series; the third, the law of development, became a concept, because the source orders an open repetition of one cycle. The author's own divisions stay orderings. Twenty-nine records that repeat a neighbour were merged into it. Two were dropped: a caution the first pass inferred from a contrast, and a prerequisite whose prior steps are no steps a person takes. The integrator also added seven nodes that both passes held only inside other records: two prerequisites with stated consequence (Matan Tora §13; Ha-Shalom §19); the nation with its needs in its own hands (Ha-Arvut §17) and the ingathering of the exiles (Ha-Shalom §20), so that the prerequisites there have their steps; and three series (Ha-Arvut §17 and §§19-20; Ha-Shalom §§19-20). Neither pass found a receiving. The work quotes and interprets, and it claims no other speaker for its own words, so no pm:Receiving is written."@en ;
    skos:note "Compared before writing with the harvests of the Petiḥa, the Haqdama le-Sefer ha-Zohar and the Haqdama le-Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot by the same author. Six records of the first pass carry claims that five nodes of those files already carry, and they are not written again; links point at those nodes. Two of the six are the practice of Torah and commandments to give contentment to the Maker (Petiḥa §10). The others are its purification of the body (Petiḥa §10), the bread of shame (Histaklut Penimit ch. 1), and the law of root and branch together with the pleasures and pains it explains (Histaklut Penimit ch. 4, two records). Twenty-six nodes state a claim of those files in a different form and carry a note beginning Differs from."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvest counts: 139 nodes. There are 85 concepts, among them 13 capacities, 18 conditions of failure, 3 signs of success and 8 disputes entered as concepts. The rest are 10 practices, 11 yieldings, 7 prerequisites (6 at stated necessity, 1 at prescribed order), 9 cautions, 9 namings, 5 series and 3 orderings. Seven nodes were written by the integrator. By the first essay they cite, 41 nodes rest on Matan Tora, 34 on Ha-Arvut and 64 on Ha-Shalom. Three signs of success stand against eighteen conditions of failure. The first sign is that one who acts for its own sake feels no difference between love of the fellow and love of God, which the author says is seen with both eyes (Matan Tora §§13-14). The second is a counterfactual: a society working for God would surpass all lands within a few years (Ha-Shalom §8). The third is the end of poverty and exploitation, announced for the future (§10). Fifteen of the conditions of failure bear on human conduct, most of them on conduct in society. They are keeping the one chair (Matan Tora §4); worship for reward (§13); one's own cares, and the few who return to self-love and hold back the people (Ha-Arvut §§17-18); the nations' refusal before the majority of merit (§21); one sin pushing the world back (§22); and service taken on for the reward (§29). In Ha-Shalom they are leaving society (§5); neglected bestowal (§6); bestowal done for humanity's sake or on a dry principle (§§8-9); nature's revenge on those who refuse (§§6, 10); paying unknowingly through suffering (§16); the political extremes (§19); and the land and the Temple that could not hold (§20). The other three are the impossibility for one person alone (Matan Tora §§3-4), the too-small number of the patriarchs' time (§16), and the failure of every system of providence to close the rift (Ha-Shalom §4). The text sets no deadline that could be counted; Ha-Shalom forecasts only that nature will prevail in the end. The work prescribes ten practices, most of them turned toward others. They are love of the neighbour at its full measure; the education not for its own sake; the mutual guarantee; each commandment for its own sake as a weight for the world; the kingdom of priests without private property; bestowal on society at its full measure; bestowal on others to give contentment to God; taking from the world as a loan; the special works that hasten the sense of evil; and taking His yoke in knowledge of God."@en .

