pmw:Zohar a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Zohar"@en, "Sohar"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run. The Petiḥa cites the Tikkunei Zohar at section 6 and the Zohar Hadash at section 32 and interprets both; those citations were read in the Petiḥa and the Zohar itself was not opened. The edition the Petiḥa was read in is the Zohar printing with Ashlag's Sulam commentary, of which the Petiḥa is the preface. The Haqdama, the other preface of that commentary, quotes the Zohar (Teruma, Emor, Mishpatim, Tazria, Noah, Naso), the Zohar Hadash and the Tikkunei Zohar (Tikkunim 6 and 30, Tikkunim Hadashim) and interprets them; it holds the Zohar to be by R. Shimon bar Yohai against those who give it to Moses de Leon (§§59-60). Those citations were read in the Haqdama; the Zohar itself was not opened."@en .

pmw:PardesRimmonim a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Moses Cordovero, Pardes Rimmonim"@en, "Mose Cordovero, Pardes Rimmonim"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run, which takes one work. Reachability not checked in this run."@en .

pmw:EtzHayyim a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ḥayyim Vital, Ez Ḥayyim"@en, "Chajim Vital, Ez Chajim"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run, which takes one work. The Petiḥa expounds its system without naming it; it names instead the author's own Panim Masbirot (section 1), which is not opened either. The Haqdama names it: it gives the purpose of creation from Ez Ḥayyim, Shaʿar ha-Kelalim, chapter 1 (§6), and refers to its own introduction to Panim Masbirot on Ez Ḥayyim (§61). The Haqdama le-Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot quotes Vital's introduction printed with Ez Ḥayyim (§19), cites its gate 48 (§§150, 152), and takes from it the Ari's words set at the head of each page of Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot (§156); those passages were read there, and Ez Ḥayyim itself was not opened. Reachability not checked in this run."@en .

pmw:ShaarHaGilgulim a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ḥayyim Vital, Šaʿar ha-Gilgulim"@en, "Chajim Vital, Schaʿar ha-Gilgulim"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run, which takes one work. Reachability not checked in this run."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:Kabbalah a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PetihaLeHokhmatHaQabbala, pmw:HaqdamaLeSeferHaZohar, pmw:HaqdamaLeTalmudEserHaSefirot, pmw:MatanToraHaArvutHaShalom ;
    rdfs:label "Kabbalah"@en, "Kabbala"@de ;
    skos:definition "The medieval, early modern and modern Jewish transmission of the sefirot as the structure of emanation, and of the human act as bearing on it."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, the first of the three decisions SPEC §14 requires, kept at documented, and the route is in the text, though not under the names one would expect. The Petiḥa names no Lurianic author or work by title: Luria, Vital and Ez Ḥayyim do not occur in it. What it names is the Talmud (Makkot 23b) and Bereshit Rabba 44 in section 1, the Tikkunei Zohar in section 6, the Zohar Hadash in section 32, the Sefer Yetzira in section 100, and the author's own Panim Masbirot, Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot with Or Penimi, and the Tree printed at the end of his Beit Shaʿar la-Kavanot (sections 1, 20, 111, 168). Its whole vocabulary — Adam Kadmon and its partzufim, Akudim, Nekudim, the breaking of the vessels, Atik, Arikh Anpin, Abba ve-Imma, Zeʿir Anpin and Nukva, raising MaN — is the Lurianic system, and the work presents itself as an exposition of it. Ashlag's training among Hasidim is not named in the text. Any agreement between this file and the Zohar, Lurianic or Hasidic material of this holding, pmt:Hasidism included, is reception along that route and counts once."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, as the Haqdama le-Sefer ha-Zohar shows it, where the Petiḥa did not: the Haqdama names the Lurianic source. It takes the purpose of creation from Ez Ḥayyim, Shaʿar ha-Kelalim 1 (§6); it says the author explained the writings of the Ari before the Zohar (§58); it names R. Isaac Luria as the soul who revealed the principles of the Zohar and of Kabbalah, dying in 5332, after whom his words stayed with a few until leave was given in the author's generation (§§61-62); and it refers to the author's introduction to Panim Masbirot on Ez Ḥayyim (§61). It holds the Zohar to be R. Shimon bar Yohai's and reports, without reading it, the view that gives it to Moses de Leon (§§59-60), and it quotes the Zohar, the Zohar Hadash, the Tikkunei Zohar and Maimonides. The route is documented in the text itself: Zohar, Luria, Ez Ḥayyim, then the author's commentaries."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, as the Haqdama le-Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot shows it: the work names the Lurianic chain itself. It quotes word for word R. Ḥayyim Vital's introduction to Shaʿar ha-Haqdamot, printed as the introduction to Ez Ḥayyim, on the order of study, and defends it (§§19-24); it cites Ez Ḥayyim, gate 48, for the four worlds (§§150, 152); it names the author's Panim Meʾirot u-Panim Masbirot on Ez Ḥayyim (§§37, 156); and it describes Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot as the Ari's words, chiefly from Ez Ḥayyim, set at the head of each page with the two commentaries Or Penimi and Histaklut Penimit (§156), Histaklut Penimit itself opening on the Ari's words from Ez Ḥayyim, first gate (ch. 1, para 1). It names the Gra's Siddur for the order from sod to peshat (§§103, 148) and the line of authors of Kabbalah books from Sefer Yetzira and the Zohar to the Gra and the Rav of Liadi (§30). The route is documented in the text: the Ari through Vital's Ez Ḥayyim to the author's commentaries."@en ;
    skos:note "Recension and edition, the second decision. The Hebrew is the text of the Sefaria library in the version Zohar with Sulam commentary, Jerusalem 1945 (version source given by Sefaria as NLI NNL_ALEPH001329911); the text layer carries no title page, so the edition rests on the version record. It was cross-read against the Bnei Baruch Hebrew, which keeps the abbreviations of the print."@en ;
    skos:note "The third decision, the number of citable places: 188 sections, numbered 1 to 188 with 99 absent from the numbering. Every node of traditions/kabbalah-petiha.ttl cites one section or a short run of them. Sections cited by no node, because they recapitulate, ask a question answered in the next section, or detail what a neighbouring node holds: 23, 36-37, 41, 61, 69-70, 73, 92, 95, 111, 114, 131-133, 136-137, 140, 145, 147, 157, and 99, which is empty. Sections 107, 164, 167, 169 and 171 have no node of their own and are cited only inside the run of an ordering or a series (the kings, §§104-109; the three ascents, §§163-173)."@en ;
    skos:note "The same decision for the Haqdama le-Sefer ha-Zohar: 71 sections, numbered by the author with Hebrew letters alef to ayin-alef, 169 paragraphs in the text layer. Every node of traditions/kabbalah-haqdama-zohar.ttl cites one section or a short run of them. Sections cited by no node of that file: 1, the five questions, which are answered and entered where the answers stand; 6 and 7, whose claims (the purpose of creation, pleasure measured by the will, the will as the one thing created from nothing) are carried by nodes of the Petiḥa and not written twice. Section 6 is cited at the tradition for its naming of Ez Ḥayyim."@en ;
    skos:note "The same decision for the Haqdama le-Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot: 155 sections of the Introduction, numbered by the author with Hebrew letters alef to qof-nun-vav with 71 absent from the numbering, 443 segments in the text layer; and Histaklut Penimit to Part 1, ten chapters in 36 paragraphs of the Sefaria segmentation. Every node of traditions/kabbalah-haqdama-tes.ttl cites one section, or one chapter and paragraph, or a short run of them. Sections cited by no node: 2, 20, 38, 40, 41, 65, 75, 76, 77, 79, 86, 92, 99, 112, 113 and 123, which ask a question that a later section answers and where the answer is entered; 46, 50, 104, 114, 119, 131, 143, 145 and 153, which recapitulate or detail what a neighbouring node holds; and 108, the parable of the servant whipped in the master's absence, which illustrates the turning of suffering into joy at §§107 and 116. Paragraphs of Histaklut Penimit cited by no node of that file: ch. 1, paras 3 and 4; ch. 2, para 1; ch. 3, para 1; ch. 6, para 1; ch. 8, para 3; ch. 10, para 1; their claims are carried by nodes of the Petiḥa and of the Haqdama le-Sefer ha-Zohar and are not written twice."@en ;
    skos:note "The same decision for the essays Matan Tora, Ha-Arvut and Ha-Shalom: 49 sections in 160 segments of the text layer. The author numbers them with Hebrew letters, Matan Tora 1 to 16, Ha-Arvut carrying on from 17 to 29, and Ha-Shalom 1 to 20 on its own. Every node of traditions/kabbalah-matan-tora.ttl cites one section of one essay or a short run of them, by the essay's name and the section. One section is cited by no node: Matan Tora §5, which asks why the Torah was given to Israel alone. The author answers it at Ha-Arvut §§23-24, and the answer is entered there. The mottos before the first section of each essay have no section of their own and are cited inside the sections that expound them: Leviticus 19:18 with Rabbi Akiva's saying, Sanhedrin 27b, and Isaiah 11 with the end of Uktzin."@en ;
    skos:note "What the reading found against the expectation of the registration's line. The Petiḥa is a systematic account of emanation from the first restriction to the worlds of Beria, Yetzira and Assiya, and almost none of it is a procedure: the human act enters at its beginning (§§1-12, 57-58), in passing at §§126 and 150-151, and at its end (§§141-176), and there it carries the work's few testable claims, that good deeds raise MaN and make the worlds rise, that spoiled deeds make them fall, and that the lights above the six extremities never become fixed for that reason. The orders it sets out are many and strict; ten are entered as series and thirteen as orderings, and none of the series names a consequence for skipping one of its places."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Sefer ha-Bahir; Zohar; Cordovero, Pardes Rimmonim; Vital, Ez Ḥayyim and Šaʿar ha-Gilgulim; Ashlag, Petiḥa le-Ḥokhmat ha-Qabbala, Haqdama le-Sefer ha-Zohar, Haqdama le-Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot, and the essays Matan Tora, Ha-Arvut, Ha-Shalom, with the further pieces of Kuntres Matan Torah" ;
